# <<.Workspace.Name>> onboarding

> **Design time.** This skill is for the coding agent working in this repo. It is
> never synced into the platform. Skills the *deployed* agent uses at runtime
> live in `common/skills/` and reach the cluster through a `SkillSet` - a
> completely separate mechanism. Do not confuse the two.

This repo defines the Asgard custom resources (`asgard-ai.com/v1alpha1`) for
<<.Workspace.Name>>, one Helm chart per project, each deployed to its own
namespace. There is no application source code.

## Start here, every session

```bash
asgard-cli next
```

It works out where the onboarding is **from the repository itself** - which files
exist, which CR kinds each chart declares - and prints that stage's guidance. It
stays right when a step was done by hand, and it answers in an empty directory,
where there is nothing yet to read.

**But it is an opinion, not an instruction.** It derives a stage from a fixed set
of signals; you can read more than that - `AGENTS.md`, the living spec, the open
questions, what the charts actually declare, and what the person in front of you
just asked. **Where you disagree with it, you are probably right**: an onboarding
is not linear, and three of this engagement's most expensive decisions were made
once, built, and reversed after contact with reality. A tool that walks you into
the same first answer is the failure mode, not the goal.

So form your own view of where things stand, and reach for the guidance by
subject rather than by position:

```bash
asgard-cli find "<what you are actually deciding>"
```

That searches the platform wiki, the deployment shapes and the stage guidance
together, so "how is the read path decided" reaches stage 4's material without
having to have arrived at stage 4.

**Do not reproduce what any of it says.** Run it and read it: the guidance is
versioned with the tool, while a copy pasted into a transcript goes stale.

## Where the material is

Not a sequence to follow - a map of what answers what.

| what you are doing | where to look |
|---|---|
| anything, first | `asgard-cli next` |
| **the customer said something and you have to turn it into work** | `asgard-cli next --stage requirements` - the interview, then `asgard-cli request add "<their words>"` |
| **looking something up** | `asgard-cli find "<the customer's words>"` - searches both bodies of reference material and hands you the other half |
| **about to write a CR** | `asgard-cli usecase <shape>` - the skeleton and its traps |
| what a thing on the platform even is | `asgard-cli wiki <page>` - read this before the extract, which assumes it |
| how to change this repo - layout, conventions, CRD traps | `AGENTS.md` |
| what the system currently does | `docs/spec/<<.SpecSlug>>/` |
| why something was decided | `docs/decisions/` |
| a `SemanticLayer` or `DataConnector`, or any query against a customer database | `semantic-layer-modeling` skill |
| before committing anything under `projects/*/chart` or `common/` | `asgard-cr-verification` skill |
| work that needs a spec before implementation | `spec-workflow` skill |
| a slide deck for the customer | `proposal-deck` skill |
| **any 繁體中文 the customer will read** - a slide, a reply to their question, a cube `description`, an agent's prompt | `plain-chinese` skill. Load it for the second pass, not while drafting |
| registering a new project | `asgard-cli project add <slug>` - it writes the chart skeleton too |
| the repo has drifted: contradictions, stale claims, a term nobody owns | `knowledge-base` skill, after `asgard-cli check` |

## The question behind the three big decisions

The read path, the entry point, and where unstructured knowledge lives all
follow from one question:

> **Who is on the other end - an internal caller that can authenticate, or an
> anonymous visitor?**

An internal audience and a public one need different shapes, and that is not a
preference: it follows from what the platform can and cannot do. Each of the
three has an obvious answer that has already been proven wrong in production.
`asgard-cli next` states the reversal when you reach that stage; `AGENTS.md` has
the same three under "The three decisions that get answered wrong".

**Establish the audience before designing anything.**

## Rules that hold at every stage

- **Never guess a schema.** Connect to the real database and introspect it.
  Reasoning about a schema is not verifying it.
- **Source systems are read-only** until a spec says otherwise. Any write path
  points outward, never at a source system, and needs human approval.
- **Run the gate before committing** anything under `projects/*/chart` or
  `common/`, and stop at the first red step.
- **Write the decision down the day it is settled** -
  `docs/decisions/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>.md`, saying why the rejected option was
  rejected. That sentence is the only part nobody can reconstruct later.
- **A behaviour change is not done** until the delta reaches
  `docs/spec/<<.SpecSlug>>/`. A task spec stops being read once it is done; the
  living spec is what the next person reads.
